const page = legacyPages['blogs-search-economics'];
---

<SiteLayout {...page} legacy={true} noindex={false}>
  <main id="main" class="article-content">
    <nav class="article-breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><a href="/blogs/"
        >Hiring &amp; career guides</a
      >
    </nav><p class="overline">Quantum Leaps · Practical guidance</p><h1>
      Why a Focused Shortlist Beats a Pile of CVs
    </h1><p class="article-meta">
      By Quantum Leaps · Published and reviewed 25 October 2026 · 2 min read
    </p><p class="article-note">
      A useful shortlist explains relevant evidence, tradeoffs and open
      questions instead of asking the hiring manager to start screening again.
    </p><h2>Define the decision before the search</h2><p>
      A shortlist is only useful when the hiring team agrees what good work
      looks like. Write down the problems the person will own, the capabilities
      needed to solve them and the constraints that cannot change. Separate
      genuine requirements from preferences.
    </p><h2>Ask what each introduction adds</h2><p>
      For each candidate, a recruiter should explain why their experience
      matters, what has been discussed and what still needs checking. A job
      title alone is not evidence of capability. A relevant project, portfolio
      decision or example of ownership can support a more useful conversation.
    </p><h2>Make tradeoffs visible</h2><p>
      One candidate may know your market but need support with a larger team.
      Another may have led at scale but be new to your sector. Describing these
      tradeoffs helps the hiring team decide what to explore rather than
      treating a score as an answer.
    </p><h2>Use the shortlist to calibrate</h2><ul>
      <li>Ask the hiring manager which evidence is compelling and why.</li><li>
        Record consistent reasons for progressing or declining a profile.
      </li><li>
        Check whether budget, location or scope is restricting the search.
      </li><li>
        Agree whether the brief needs adjustment before expanding volume.
      </li>
    </ul><p>
      Three profiles is a useful illustration of focus, not a universal quota.
      The appropriate shortlist size and timeline depend on the role and the
      available market.
    </p><p>
      <a href="/contact/">Discuss a hiring brief →</a> · <a href="/submit-cv/"
        >Share your CV →</a
      >
    </p>
  </main>
</SiteLayout>
